Dockerfile fundamentals 

After working on containerizing a few services recently, I realized how often teams overlook the basics that actually make or break a reliable image.

 Here’s how I think about the key Dockerfile instructions — and where they matter in real projects.
 
FROM — defines the base environment. In production you might pick python:3.12-slim or node:20-alpine to start small, or ubuntu when you need package flexibility.
RUN — used for installing packages and setting up dependencies during build.
 Example: installing system tools or Python libraries your app needs before deployment.
COPY — moves your local source code and configs into the image.
 Example: copying Flask or Node app files into /app before starting the server.
ADD — similar to COPY but can extract archives or fetch URLs.
 Rare in CI/CD pipelines unless you’re unpacking release bundles.
ENV — sets environment variables that apps use at runtime.
 Example: ENV PORT=8080 or ENV APP_ENV=production so the same image runs across dev, staging, and prod.
WORKDIR — defines the default working directory for subsequent commands.
 Used to keep code and commands organized (/app is a common choice).
EXPOSE — documents which port your container listens on.
 Example: EXPOSE 8000 for a web API — helps Compose or Kubernetes map traffic correctly.
USER — drops root privileges and runs the app safely as a normal user.
 Used in security-hardened builds or in production workloads.
CMD — sets the default command when the container starts.
 Example: CMD ["python", "app.py"] — the process that runs when the container launches.
ENTRYPOINT — defines the main executable that always runs, even if arguments are passed.
 Example: useful for CLI tools or scripts where you want a fixed entry command.
 
Real-world habits that help:
Always add a .dockerignore to avoid copying unnecessary files (git history, logs, venvs).
Use multi-stage builds to keep final images small and secure.
Pin versions and prefer slim or alpine variants to reduce CVEs.
Drop root access with USER whenever possible.
These fundamentals form the base of every production Dockerfile — from simple Python APIs to multi-stage Go and Node builds deployed through CI/CD pipelines.
